Good question — this is a classic EF Core configuration issue, not a problem with your stored procedure logic 👍
Your code is mostly correct, but the error tells us exactly what’s wrong.


---

❌ ERROR EXPLAINED

> InvalidOperationException: The ConnectionString property has not been initialized.



This means:

➡️ Database.GetDbConnection() was created
➡️ BUT EF Core was never given a connection string

So when this line runs:

using var conn = Database.GetDbConnection();
await conn.OpenAsync();

EF Core has no idea which SQL Server to connect to.


---

✅ ROOT CAUSE (99% THIS)

Your ApplicationDbContext is not configured with UseSqlServer(...)
OR it is not registered correctly in Program.cs.


---

✅ FIX 1 (MOST IMPORTANT): Check Program.cs

You MUST have this 👇

builder.Services.AddDbContext<ApplicationDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(
        builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection")
    ));

✔ Without this → connection string is NULL
✔ With this → Database.GetDbConnection() works


---

✅ FIX 2: Check appsettings.json

Make sure you have:

{
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection": "Server=YOUR_SERVER;Database=YOUR_DB;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True;"
  }
}

⚠️ Common mistakes:

Typo in ConnectionStrings

Typo in DefaultConnection

Empty value



---

✅ FIX 3: Verify Constructor (YOURS IS OK ✅)

Your constructor is correct:

public ApplicationDbContext(DbContextOptions<ApplicationDbContext> options)
    : base(options)
{
}

❌ DO NOT add a parameterless constructor
❌ DO NOT manually new-up DbContext


---

⚠️ BUG IN YOUR GetTotalScansAsync (IMPORTANT)

You open the connection manually but never close it.

❌ Current Code

using var conn = Database.GetDbConnection();
await conn.OpenAsync();

using var cmd = conn.CreateCommand();
cmd.CommandText = "sp_Students_GetTotalScans";
cmd.CommandType = CommandType.StoredProcedure;

var result = await cmd.ExecuteScalarAsync();
return Convert.ToInt32(result);


---

✅ FIXED VERSION (SAFE)

public async Task<int> GetTotalScansAsync()
{
    await using var conn = Database.GetDbConnection();

    if (conn.State != ConnectionState.Open)
        await conn.OpenAsync();

    await using var cmd = conn.CreateCommand();
    cmd.CommandText = "sp_Students_GetTotalScans";
    cmd.CommandType = CommandType.StoredProcedure;

    var result = await cmd.ExecuteScalarAsync();
    return Convert.ToInt32(result);
}

EF Core will now:

✔ Use the configured connection string

✔ Open safely

✔ Dispose correctly



---

✅ WHY OTHER METHODS WORK BUT THIS FAILS

These work:

FromSqlRaw(...)
ExecuteSqlRawAsync(...)

Because EF Core internally manages the connection.

This fails:

Database.GetDbConnection()

Because YOU are now responsible for the connection — and EF Core needs to be configured first.


